# What `pub publish` leaves out of the `jet_print` archive.
#
# `.pubignore` REPLACES `.gitignore` for packaging: where this file is present,
# pub reads it instead of the `.gitignore` in the same directory when deciding
# what goes into the archive. It therefore has to list everything that should
# stay out, including paths git already ignores — nothing is inherited from the
# `.gitignore` it displaces. (There is no `.gitignore` in this directory today;
# the repository-root one is unaffected, and git's view of the tree does not
# change.) The syntax is `.gitignore` syntax and paths are relative to this
# package directory.
#
# Safety check behind the exclusions below: the bundled default font is
# embedded as base64 in `lib/src/rendering/text/fonts/default_font_data.dart`,
# generated offline from `tool/fonts/`. `pubspec.yaml` declares no `assets:`
# and no `fonts:` section, and nothing under `lib/` reads a `.ttf`, a bundle or
# `tool/` at run time, so excluding `tool/` removes a build-time input only.
#
# `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `LICENSE`, `lib/`, `pubspec.yaml` and
# `l10n.yaml` are deliberately NOT listed: pana scores the first three and the
# rest are what a consumer resolves against.

# Build output and local tool state. Large, machine-specific, never consumed by
# a package consumer.
build/
.dart_tool/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies

# The test suite, including PNG/PDF goldens and fixtures — several megabytes,
# and the bulk of the archive. A consumer cannot run it: the architecture
# guards resolve paths from the workspace root, which the archive does not
# contain. pana does not score it either.
test/
dart_test.yaml

# Font-subsetting tooling: `generate_default_font.dart` plus the four committed
# Noto Sans TTF subsets it reads. Development inputs, already baked into
# `lib/` as base64 (see the note above) — not loaded at run time.
tool/

# The example ships: it is worth 10 pana points and is the first thing an
# evaluator opens. Do not add `example/` here to shrink the archive.
# Its lockfile does not ship, though — `.pubignore` replaces the repository's
# `.gitignore` for packaging, so the ignore rule there does not reach it.
example/pubspec.lock
